\title{entropiaR: Tidy Interface to EntropIA SQLite Databases}
\description{
Tidyverse-style, read-only interface between R and the SQLite database generated by the EntropIA desktop application. Provides lazy table access via 'dbplyr', typed collection with automatic timestamp and JSON handling, parameter-safe full-text search, corpus and metadata helpers, corpus quality diagnostics, analysis helpers, and reproducible export with provenance.
}
\details{
\subsection{Lifecycle}{

The public API follows the \href{https://lifecycle.r-lib.org/}{lifecycle} stages.
New capabilities ship as \code{experimental} and stabilise in later releases. In
v1 the analysis layer is experimental: \code{\link[=entropia_temporal_profile]{entropia_temporal_profile()}},
\code{\link[=entropia_document_lengths]{entropia_document_lengths()}}, \code{\link[=entropia_entity_frequency]{entropia_entity_frequency()}},
\code{\link[=entropia_topic_frequency]{entropia_topic_frequency()}}, \code{\link[=entropia_compare_collections]{entropia_compare_collections()}} and
\code{\link[=entropia_analysis_dataset]{entropia_analysis_dataset()}} carry the experimental badge and are expected
to stabilise in v2.

Deprecations use \code{\link[lifecycle:deprecate_soft]{lifecycle::deprecate_warn()}} with a one-release grace
period: a function deprecated in release N keeps working (with a warning)
through release N+1 and is removed in N+2. Deprecation warnings carry a
stable condition class so tests and user code can catch them.

The backward-compatibility surface is the column-contract manifest
(\code{inst/schemas/manifest.json}) plus the schema compatibility policy
(\code{options(entropiaR.schema_policy)}): schema support is removed only by
bumping the manifest, never silently. Error and warning conditions follow
the same contract as functions: every exported message is cli-formatted,
carries a stable class, and lists an actionable next step.
}

\subsection{Writing a deprecation}{

When a function or argument is superseded, signal the deprecation through
the package's internal wrapper \code{ent_deprecate()}, which forwards to
\code{lifecycle::deprecate_warn()} so users get the standard staged warning:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{ent_deprecate(
  when = "0.1.0",
  what = "entropia_old_function()",
  with = "entropia_new_function()"
)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The \code{when} version is the release that deprecates, \code{what} is the deprecated
object (suffix the name with \verb{()} for a function), and \code{with} names the
replacement. Keep the function working for the one-release grace period,
then remove it in the following release. Deprecation warnings carry the
standard \code{lifecycle_warning_deprecated} condition class, so tests and user
code can catch them.
}
